"1. BMC Prim Care. 2025 Dec 22;26(1):401. doi: 10.1186/s12875-025-03071-x.\n\nKnowledge, attitude, and practice towards insulin therapy in type 2 diabetes: an \ninsight from Ethiopian context.\n\nSheno BW(1), Goush AR(2), Meshesha MD(3).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Internal Medicine, Dilla University, Dilla, Ethiopia. \nbiniyamw2@gmail.com.\n(2)Department of Internal Medicine, Addis Ababa University, Addis Ababa, \nEthiopia.\n(3)Department of Internal Medicine, Dilla University, Dilla, Ethiopia.\n\nBACKGROUND: As type 2 diabetes is a growing health problem worldwide, targeting \nan early treatment and intensification of therapy will decrease the associated \nmorbidity and mortality as evidenced by large trials. Insulin therapy is delayed \nin this group of patients and multiple physician and patient factors have been \nmentioned in different settings. METHODS: A cross-sectional, hospital-based, \nquantitative study was conducted among 221 people with type 2 diabetes attending \nTikur Anbessa Specialized Hospital endocrine clinic and 122 physicians who were \nworking at this clinic. The study was conducted from July 1, 2020 to September \n30, 2020. RESULT: About 31.5% of the patients reported taking insulin at the \nprescribed time was difficult while this difficulty was mentioned by 76% of the \nphysicians. Hypoglycemia was found to be one of the most feared parts of having \ndiabetes in 81.1% of patients on Insulin therapy. 87% of physicians and 76.6% of \npatients think insulin-treated diabetes controls life while. 56% of patients on \nnon insulin therapy know nothing about insulin therapy. 23% of patients said \nthey will not accept insulin therapy the top reason being they will be afraid of \ninsulin injection because they think it will be painful. CONCLUSION: Physicians \ntend to overestimate the difficulty of insulin injection preparation and dose \nadjustment was feared for the risk of hypoglycemia when starting insulin \ntherapy. More than half of the patients who did not initiate insulin therapy \nknow nothing about insulin therapy and this needs to improve. Even if patients \nnot on insulin therapy overestimate insulin needle phobia and injection pain \nthose who are already on insulin disagreed with them.\n\nDOI: 10.1186/s12875-025-03071-x\nPMCID: PMC12723896\nPMID: 41430125 [Indexed for MEDLINE]\n\nConflict of interest statement: Declarations. Ethics approval and consent to \nparticipate: The protocol approvals are obtained from the ethical review \ncommittee of the Hospital and the department of Internal Medicine. Written \ninformed consent to participate in the study was obtained from all the \nparticipants. Confidentiality and privacy were kept for all of the participants. \nCompeting interests: The authors declare no competing interests. Consent to \npublish: All authors consented for the publication."